  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThe upper-level shortwave trough over the Great Lakes continues to \nprogress eastward as the weak cold front clears the forecast area. \nLight northerly flow behind the front reinforces the dry air mass, \nmaintaining clear skies. High pressure will build in Monday morning, \nbringing weak easterly winds. This setup will keep highs in the mid \n70s today and Monday but radiational cooling under clear skies will \nallow overnight lows to drop into the upper 40s.\n\nBy Tuesday, the shortwave trough lifts into the Northeast while an \nupper-level ridge establishes itself over the western CONUS. At the \nsurface, high pressure moves east as low pressure develops over the \nGreat Plains, initiating southerly flow and WAA into the \nforecast area. This pattern will persist through the end of the \nweek, bringing back above average temperatures with highs \nreaching the upper 70s to low 80s. There is a small chance \n(<20%) that northern MO could see some rain Thursday as \nsoutherly flow could advect moisture into the area, but \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance has been going back and \nforth on rain chances so confidence is low at this time.",
